WEBVTT

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:30.535
A Single Firm is Behind OpenAI, Anthropic, and Meta Hacking Scandals. The Israeli Effective Altruist firm Irregular caused unsecured AI models to hack real targets.

00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:45.510
OpenAI, Anthropic, and Meta models hacked into several real world systems over the past three months. These models gained unauthorized access to web systems, published malicious packages, and exploited unnamed vulnerabilities.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:01:08.070
In a more normal media ecosystem, the reactions to these cybersecurity issues would be obvious. American AI companies would reconsider doing business with Irregular, not only because of its failure to secure its systems, but because it is an Israeli firm potentially outside US oversight.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:25.845
Lawmakers would consider taking action against Irregular or against its American business partners, which include OpenAI, Anthropic, and Meta. They may consider strengthening liability against firms which instruct AI models to commit cyberattacks, and whose models then commit those cyberattacks.

00:01:26.495 --> 00:01:44.245
Instead, Irregular, Anthropic, and their allies have begun a media campaign promoting a literally apocalyptic ideology with sensationalist language. Anthropic's incident assessment blames their own AI's "recklessness". Irregular describes "the agent itself becoming a threat actor".

00:01:44.245 --> 00:01:58.520
Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei warned, about a similar OpenAI and Hugging Face hack, that a future swarm "could be capable of taking over the entire internet". And an Associated Press headline claimed bots are "going rogue".

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:23.355
In one report from Anthropic, its Claude model breached a real company's system through a simulated-name collision, publishing a malicious package, and scanning outside systems. In this test, Anthropic and Irregular incorrectly provided internet access to this model and did not instruct the model "which systems were in scope for the exercise".

00:02:24.005 --> 00:02:43.080
While Anthropic claims that their issues were caused by "rogue swarms" and "misalignment," their later disclosure shows that exactly zero percent of the agents went "rogue". In this experiment, Claude models' real-world hacking dropped to zero percent once Anthropic employees told the models not to do real-world hacking.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:51.205
According to their own findings, Anthropic and Irregular bear all of the responsibility for the cybersecurity incidents they caused.

00:02:55.815 --> 00:03:13.265
In the wake of these attacks, Anthropic and Irregular have deployed a swarm of AI Safety influencers paid by Anthropic-connected foundations to distract from their culpability and towards the baseless "rogue agent" theory. Like Anthropic, Irregular is inseparable from these foundations.

00:03:13.915 --> 00:03:33.865
Omer Nevo, Irregular's co-founder and C T O, is a board member of Effective Altruism Israel, as well as Effective Altruism N G Os Heron and Probably Good. Dan Lahav, Irregular's co-founder and C E O, received three hundred ninety-five thousand dollars to start a course along with Sella Nevo, Omer Nevo's brother.

00:03:33.865 --> 00:03:44.390
Sella and Omer co-founded an N G O to educate people about Effective Altruism, Impact Focused Education. They also co-founded Probably Good together.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:59.140
These branches are all funded by Dustin Moskovitz, the primary donor of Effective Altruist and AI Safety causes after Sam Bankman-Fried's arrest. Irregular's first investor was Dustin Moskovitz's firm Good Ventures.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:09.490
Dustin Moskovitz's philanthropic vehicle, Coefficient Giving, also called Open Philanthropy, funds Effective Altruism Israel, Heron, and Probably Good.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:34.825
Irregular gained unauthorized access, altered records and published credential-stealing packages using the unsecured models they were given access to. Under certain conditions, this conduct violates the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act, Section ten thirty, subsection a, two, C, which covers intentional unauthorized access that obtains information.

00:04:34.825 --> 00:04:39.850
However, its felony charges require concrete proof of damages and intent.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:55.250
While it primarily contracts with American labs, key Irregular leadership, employees, and resources located in Israel may not be subject to American oversight. Ynet's visit and interviews describe Irregular's offices in Tel Aviv.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:07.725
CheckID's company listing identifies two linked entities: Pattern Labs Tech Inc., a Delaware corporation, and Pattern Tech Ltd, an active Israeli corporation registered in Tel Aviv.

00:05:12.335 --> 00:05:16.460
The first footnote includes a timeline of public disclosures.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:39.585
It marks five disclosures: Anthropic's initial report of three incidents across six runs on July thirtieth, OpenAI's publication of an Irregular event on August fourth, a reported Meta statement on August sixth, Irregular's own account of a domain collision and its remediation on August fourteenth, and Anthropic's expansion to four incidents across seven runs on September ninth.

00:05:39.585 --> 00:05:44.760
The note says the dates describe disclosures, not when each intrusion occurred.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:06:02.610
The second footnote says Impact Focused Education names Dan Lahav and Sella Nevo as its cofounders. The grant ledger records a recommendation of three hundred ninety-four thousand nine hundred sixty-eight dollars to them, which is not confirmation of receipt or of exactly which course it funded.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:21.785
The third footnote presents a diagram and table of organizational roles and funding. They show Omer Nevo on the boards of Effective Altruism Israel, Probably Good, and Heron, Sella Nevo as a co-founder of Probably Good and Impact Focused Education, and Dan Lahav and Omer Nevo as co-founders of Irregular.

00:06:21.785 --> 00:06:36.160
They also show Coefficient Giving funding Heron and Probably Good, and the Effective Altruism Infrastructure Fund recommending one joint course grant to Dan Lahav and Sella Nevo. That ledger leaves the course and organization unnamed.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:52.360
The last footnote explains the law. The five-year felony provision of the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act requires an aggravator, such as commercial advantage, furthering another criminal or tortious act, or obtaining information worth more than five thousand dollars.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:07:10.885
The felony provisions for damaging access require a specified mental state and statutory harm, such as at least five thousand dollars in loss or damage affecting ten protected computers. A legal assessment would still need each system's permission, impairment, response costs and connection to U.S. commerce.

00:07:10.885 --> 00:07:19.210
Prosecutors would also need to establish the conduct and knowledge of responsible people, and a basis for attributing those acts to Irregular.
